Right. Memory is not a single vault in the hippocampus. The hippocampus is more like an indexing, binding, and routing hub—especially important for forming and reconstructing episodic memories—but the actual information is distributed across many systems.

The “web” picture is much closer. A single memory can recruit visual cortex for imagery, auditory cortex for sound, motor and cerebellar circuits for movement, amygdala for emotional salience, striatum for habits, prefrontal cortex for organization and retrieval, and association cortex for meaning. Over time, some memories become less dependent on the hippocampus as cortical networks strengthen their own connections.

Your aqueduct metaphor also works if we mean communication pathways: white-matter tracts, thalamocortical loops, neuromodulatory systems, autonomic pathways, and recurrent cortical circuits move and coordinate information between regions. But the literal cerebral aqueduct is mainly a cerebrospinal-fluid passage, not a memory conduit.

So a better architecture is:

nodes + webs + routes + state

The nodes specialize, the webs distribute representations, the routes coordinate traffic, and the body/chemical state changes what gets encoded and retrieved.

That is why “where is a memory?” often has no single anatomical answer. A memory is closer to a temporarily reassembled network state than a file sitting in one location.